⚠️ THE DISCRETE HASH COVERS ONLY DISCRETE STATE. Two peers cannot agree bit
for bit on a position or a clock; they must agree on who is who, who is
taya, what each tsinelas is doing and what the score is.

⚠️⚠️ AND IT IS NOT AN EQUALITY GATE ACROSS PEERS, WHICH THE STRUCTURAL HASH
IS. Discrete is not the same as CONSTANT: the score, the slipper states and
the defender all move during a match, and two reports stop at two different
instants by construction (a referee has to outlive its clients). Comparing
the discrete hash between peers therefore fails on a working link roughly
whenever anything happens. The structural hash covers only what CANNOT
change while a match runs: which character sits in which seat, which seats
are bots, and the protocol. Two peers disagreeing on that are playing two
different matches, whenever you look.
